      experimentation: [74, 0.64, "d", "A"],
      // [src_mccartney_npr_martin, src_mccartney_bb_yesterday, src_mccartney_bb_eleanor, src_mccartney_musicradar_emerick | 1965-1969 | producer + engineer + session documentation] Martin credits the Sgt. Pepper 'fictitious band' idea to McCartney (hedged: 'I think'); Yesterday was recorded with only his guitar, his voice and a string quartet, a first for the Beatles; Eleanor Rigby was built on a minimal piano vamp with a string octet; Emerick says a concept from McCartney tied the Abbey Road medley together. A pattern of format-breaking ideas, but several come through his own collaborators' framing and Martin hedges, so no higher.
      creative_originality: [74, 0.62, "s", "A"],
      // [src_mccartney_guitarworld_something, src_mccartney_bb_yesterday, src_mccartney_guitarworld_abbeyroad | 1965-1969 | press + his own recollection + engineer] Guitar World describes him as approaching the bass 'as a songwriter, and not as an instrumentalist', and McCartney says he is a melodist who tries to get a melody out of the bass part; he insisted on no vibrato from the Yesterday quartet and pushed for a seventh in the cello part; the engineer notes the new console let his bass lines move in counterpoint to the vocal. Consistent but largely his own framing and one engineer's description, so moderate confidence.
      aesthetic_sensitivity: [72, 0.58, "s", "A"],
